+
+## PU.58 - trim the strip to its ink band before the pitch is computed (2026-09-22)
+
+PU.55 re-scoped: its clip guard could not see the defect because the defect is the box's
+**vertical** extent, and the read half is a band trim, not a left/right edge invariant. This
+round trims the band the pitch and the run threshold are taken over.
+
+### The reproduction, before anything was built
+
+Under the PU.48 candidate (`ml/pump-reader/.out/det/pu48/DigitRows-pu48.mlmodel`, `d18531eb…`),
+`pump-092`'s litres window, holding one axis to the oracle and the other to the detector:
+
+| quad | horizontal | vertical | litres cells | band | pitch |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| widened box | detector | detector | **3** | 6..95 | 58 |
+| widened LR + oracle H | detector | oracle | **4** | 0..87 | 68 |
+| oracle LR + raw H | oracle | detector | **3** | 6..95 | 57 |
+| oracle | oracle | oracle | **4** | 7..87 | 68 |
+
+The litres box is `0.4709..0.6717` raw (`0.4629..0.6797` after the widening) against the oracle's
+`0.5035..0.6944`; vertically `0.4407..0.5476` against `0.4406..0.5309`, 21 % taller. The detector
+box reaches the panel bezel below the digits, the plain row-sum band runs to the strip's bottom
+edge, and the pitch and the run threshold are taken over the bezel. PU.55 measured the same four
+slices; the "raw box" row there is the widened box the live path actually slices.
+
+### What was built, and why at the slice
+
+`PumpGlyphSlicer.trimmedToInkBand`: the plain band is walked inward from each edge past the rows
+that are **solid across the strip** - a bezel runs through the gaps between glyphs, a glyph row
+leaves them at the panel's level - then widened by a margin that never re-crosses a solid row. The
+column profile, the run threshold and the pitch are computed on the trimmed band, and the cells are
+laid on it.
+
+At the slice, not at the warp. The trim changes only which rows the pitch is measured over; the
+warped strip and the quad are unchanged, so the cell rects stay in the strip's frame and every
+caller (`PumpRowGeometry`, `cropCell`, the harness) is untouched. Trimming the quad would need a
+second warp and a map from strip rows back to image points, and would move the cell rects with it.
+
+The solid rows are read from the LCN'd ink, not the raw grayscale. A raw read is robust to a tall
+bezel (the LCN flattens a strip that is mostly bezel) but fires on tight oracle strips whose glyph
+rows reach 0.6 of the width: count 227/251. The LCN'd read separates pump-092's bezel from its
+glyphs cleanly and holds the harness.
+
+### Constants, and the measurement each came from
+
+| constant | value | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| `bandTrimSolidFraction` | 0.55 | pump-092's litres bezel rows 88..95 run 0.55-0.85 of the width, its glyph rows at most 0.35; kept above 0.5 because `PumpGlyphSlicerTests`' three-glyph synthetic row carries exactly 0.5 |
+| `bandTrimMarginFraction` | 0.03 | a margin of the plain band height, so a glyph's anti-aliased edge rows stay |
+| `bandTrimMinimumHeight` | 6 | below this the cut core is noise, not a glyph band, and the plain band stands |
+| `bandTrim` | true | the mutation seam (see below) |
+
